A||||1||PaUL||Door Fix
Z||000000||PaUL||08-09-99||05:59 PM||||I just changed the door hinge pin bushings this past weekend and I would like to relay some of my observations. After reading the suggestions given to me in an earlier post, I decided to try and remove the lower pin again. Last time I couldnt get it to budge. Using a floor jack, I lifted the open door a few inches. The trick here is to lift the door just enough so the pin doesnt bind. I used a pair of locking plier on the pin to test if it was binding. I tried to turn the pin as I jacked the door up slowly. When the pin turned easily, I knew this was the point of minimum resistance. Using a hammer and long socket extension, I was able to easily drive the pin out. Theres always a trick to these things, you just need to figure it out. <p>After removing both upper and lower pins, I noticed that most of wear occurs on the lower bushing of each pin. The pin itself wears very little. The best thing to do would be to just replace the bushings and not the pin. I used a pin and bushing set from the Help line of products as was suggested. If anyone else is planning to do this, make sure that you get the bushings with the splines. The set I had came with 2 types of bushings and the non splined ones were way too loose. <p>Now with the new bushings my door closes fully and easily on the first try. Before I had to slam it shut. I thought this was the legacy of Roger Smith, ever heard of him or seen the movie Roger and Me? If you didnt, he was the CEO of GM in the 80s and lots of the companies problems were blamed on him.
Z||000001||theogre||08-09-99||08:42 PM||||for those who don't have the part, or time to dink with the dealers,,,,,,<p>The hinge pin kit is MotorMite HELP! part # 38407.(Those red blister packs strewn all over the store.) You need 2 kits per door, each has 1 pin, and 2 splined bushings. the 2 kits are &lt;10$us, and are available at nearly any parts house.
